; 45-worked-proof.kurt
;
; Every earlier lesson taught one keyword in isolation. This one is different: it walks
; through actually writing a non-trivial proof from a blank file, the way you'd really do
; it -- including a mistake and how to read the error it produces. The goal is one direction
; of de Morgan's law: ¬(p ∧ q) ⇒ ¬p ∨ ¬q. (Both directions, and the second law, are proved in
; full in proofs/natural-deduction/de-morgan.kurt if you want the complete picture afterward.)

; Step 1: what do you need loaded? The goal mentions `not`, `and`, `or` -- `and` is
; hard-coded (minimal.kurt), but `not`/`or` and their inference rules live in `prop.kurt`.
load prop
bool p, q

; Step 2: state the goal with `show`, then think about a *strategy* before writing any
; proof steps. Kurt can't search for a proof for you (see §11 "known gaps" in
; doc/kurt-doc.md: derivation is single-hop, one `impl_elim` application at a time) -- you
; supply the outline, kurt only checks each step is a real, valid inference.
;
; Strategy for ¬(p ∧ q) ⇒ ¬p ∨ ¬q: assume the antecedent, then case-split on `p ∨ ¬p`
; (excluded middle, not built in -- you have to `use` it). In the `p` case, `p ∧ q` would
; need `q` too, but if `q` also held you'd have `p ∧ q`, contradicting the assumption --
; so `¬q`, hence `¬p ∨ ¬q`. In the `¬p` case, `¬p ∨ ¬q` follows immediately from `¬p`.
use p ∨ ¬p "excluded-middle"

show ¬(p ∧ q) ⇒ ¬p ∨ ¬q
proof
    assume ¬(p ∧ q)
        ; Step 3: here's the mistake -- jumping straight to the conclusion. It's *true*
        ; here, but nothing derivable in one step from `¬(p ∧ q)` alone gets you there;
        ; kurt only checks real inferences, it doesn't take your word for a true-but-
        ; unjustified restatement (`expect` can only wrap an ordinary statement like this
        ; one, not a block-opening keyword like the `case` below, which is why this
        ; deliberate mistake has to be a bare line rather than the actual wrong case split):
        expect "ProofError"
            ¬p ∨ ¬q

        ; the error is "can not derive `¬p ∨ ¬q`" -- true, but not a one-step inference from
        ; what's in scope (`¬(p ∧ q)`). What's missing is the case split the strategy called
        ; for, and within each case, the actual contradiction that justifies it: `p` together
        ; with `¬(p ∧ q)` should give `¬q` first (via a nested `assume q` that derives
        ; `false`), and *then* `¬p ∨ ¬q` follows from `¬q` via "or-intro". Spelling out that
        ; intermediate step is exactly what single-hop derivation requires of you:
        case p
            assume q
                p ∧ q       ; and-intro, from `p` and this `assume`
                false       ; bottom-intro, contradicts `¬(p ∧ q)`
            ¬q              ; not-intro: closing the `assume q` block above
            ¬p ∨ ¬q         ; or-intro, from `¬q`
        case ¬p
            ¬p ∨ ¬q         ; or-intro, straight from `¬p`
        ¬p ∨ ¬q             ; or-elim: both cases reached the same conclusion
qed

; that's the whole method: state the goal, sketch a strategy in prose (comments) before
; writing any kurt, expect to hit "can not derive" at least once, and read *which* step
; failed to find the missing link -- almost always a contradiction, an elimination rule, or
; an intermediate restatement that needs to be spelled out explicitly. `sandbox` (15-sandbox)
; is the right place to try a step you're not sure about without committing to it.
